In my IB HL Physics class, we dropped a steel ball into a tall cylinder of glycerol as a demo. I expected it to just sink to the bottom. Instead it slowed down almost immediately and then fell at a constant speed for the rest of the drop. That surprised me enough that I wanted to actually understand why, rather than just accept "drag does that."
Looking into it led me to Stokes' Law, which predicts the drag force on a sphere moving through a viscous fluid. Rearranging it for terminal velocity showed that radius should matter a lot, velocity scales with the square of the radius, not linearly. That was the part I wanted to test for myself: if I used ball bearings of different sizes, would the relationship actually hold, and would it hold for every size, or break down somewhere?
That question turned into this investigation  using video tracking to measure terminal velocity across a range of ball bearing sizes, extracting glycerol's viscosity from the data, and then digging into why the largest balls didn't follow the predicted trend (turns out it's a mix of rising Reynolds number and the container walls interfering with the flow, confirmed by literally testing the second effect with a narrower tube).

